1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,400
You are watching the sea trial of a new ship. For the first time she is being put to the test.

2
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:31,420
test. Many tests. Zigzag turns and spiral steering. Maximum power, full speed ahead.

3
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:40,380
A sudden reverse to full speed astern. But the sea trial of this new vessel tests more

4
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:49,320
than routine handling. For this is a very special ship. The first one of her kind. The only one of

5
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:58,620
her kind today. What makes her different? Perhaps you've noticed one clue. No smokestack. A ship

6
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:06,939
without a smokestack. How can this be? What does she burn? What makes her go? The answer to these

7
00:01:06,939 --> 00:01:14,640
questions, the reason for this ship's unique value lies behind this insignia.

8
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:19,359
Beyond this symbol, released from within a special furnace called a nuclear

9
00:01:19,359 --> 00:01:26,799
reactor, lies the enormous energy of the tiny atom. Energy which can propel her

10
00:01:26,799 --> 00:01:33,560
14 times around the world on a single fuel loading. Energy which is silently,

11
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:39,740
safely, and instantly controlled through the flick of a switch, the turn of a knob.

12
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:47,280
Yes, this ship is truly different, for this is the world's first atomic-powered merchantman.

13
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:51,219
This is the nuclear ship, Savannah.

14
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:26,560
The first cargo passenger ship to be powered by nuclear energy, the NS Savannah, is a true

15
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:34,379
pioneer. For she brings to the seafaring world new flexibility and freedom. But this modern

16
00:02:34,379 --> 00:02:40,640
pioneer had her counterpart in history. A brave but tiny vessel which she honors in

17
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:49,219
carrying this golden model and in bearing her name, Savannah. This was the SS Savannah,

18
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:55,980
first to cross the ocean using steam. When Captain Moses Rogers sailed this ship from

19
00:02:55,979 --> 00:03:02,399
her home port of Savannah, Georgia on May 22nd, 1819, the cheers of many were ringing in his ears.

20
00:03:06,019 --> 00:03:10,359
But in spite of her smartly trimmed accommodations, there were no passengers.

21
00:03:11,799 --> 00:03:16,000
Who would dare sail from the site of land next to a boiler full of steam?

22
00:03:17,139 --> 00:03:18,399
And there was hardly any cargo.

23
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:25,319
Wooden coal to feed the hungry engine took up almost every bit of space.

24
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:38,320
Her 25-foot paddle wheels were retractable, withdrawn to survive the pounding of a heavy

25
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:41,800
sea.

26
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:47,000
Her smokestacks swiveled to deflect its sparks from her sails.

27
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,980
This was the first Savannah, introducing a new era of power in America's conquest of

28
00:03:51,980 --> 00:04:01,700
the sea through the years the use of steam power was to grow great effort and

29
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:05,360
ingenuity went into the design and construction of new ways to harness and

30
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:12,440
control steam the paddle wheel gave way to the propeller engines grew in size

31
00:04:12,439 --> 00:04:24,279
and power changed in design. The fuels, wood and coal, gave way to oil.

32
00:04:26,519 --> 00:04:33,139
As America's fleets changed in type and tonnage, her ships increased in size and

33
00:04:33,139 --> 00:04:38,819
speed and her growing merchant marine, providing increased trade and commerce,

34
00:04:38,819 --> 00:04:47,819
became a vital force, improving the nation's economic life, her way of life,

35
00:04:47,819 --> 00:04:53,639
and her relationship to the world.

36
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:03,860
Then came nuclear power. Men planned and worked toward the use of this new source

37
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:08,579
of power. The tremendous heat which could be released from the splitting of the

38
00:05:08,579 --> 00:05:19,579
atom. Nuclear energy. Here was a fuel ideally suited to ship propulsion. A source of energy

39
00:05:19,579 --> 00:05:25,639
which measured in pounds instead of tons could send liners around the globe, deliver shiploads

40
00:05:25,639 --> 00:05:33,039
of cargo directly to the most distant ports. With the birth of the atomic age, this dream

41
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:40,860
was to become a reality. Within an atomic furnace, the tremendous power bound up in

42
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:48,400
the nucleus of an atom could be released and controlled, and certain types of reactors

43
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:54,379
could be successfully placed into a ship. The first merchant ship to sail onto this

44
00:05:54,379 --> 00:06:01,759
new and mighty power would be America's seafaring showcase of the peaceful atom. She would be

45
00:06:01,759 --> 00:06:08,240
the nuclear ship savannah. Proposed by former President Eisenhower, the savannah's construction

46
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:14,740
was subsequently authorized by Congress in 1956. She was to be a prototype of nuclear merchant

47
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:22,139
ships and a demonstration of the dependability and safety of atomic power at sea. Her design

48
00:06:22,139 --> 00:06:27,360
was of utmost importance. The most exacting standards of both the Maritime Administration

49
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:30,100
and Atomic Energy Commission, would have to be met.

50
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:35,879
The positioning of her reactor received careful consideration.

51
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:42,379
It would have to be easily accessible, yet completely isolated by its heavy shielding.

52
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:49,720
All these requirements were amply met by George G. Sharp, Incorporated,

53
00:06:50,199 --> 00:06:55,160
in the design of a ship that would be as sleek and attractive as she was efficient and safe.

54
00:06:55,160 --> 00:07:04,080
A ship that would accommodate 60 passengers and carry 10,000 tons of cargo.

55
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:07,340
Construction of the Savannah by the New York Shipbuilding Corporation began at Camden,

56
00:07:07,340 --> 00:07:11,640
New Jersey.

57
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:16,120
The keel laying of America's first nuclear merchant ship was a significant and festive

58
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:21,320
occasion.

59
00:07:21,319 --> 00:07:25,420
With the ship's construction underway, work also began on the manufacture of her reactor

60
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:31,079
by the Babcock and Wilcox Company.

61
00:07:31,079 --> 00:07:38,939
The molding of the reactor head was one of the first steps.

62
00:07:38,939 --> 00:07:47,199
The uranium oxide, which would be the Savannah's fuel, was processed and made into pellets.

63
00:07:47,199 --> 00:07:52,779
Almost 700,000 of these pellets would be loaded into the reactor's core, enough potential

64
00:07:52,779 --> 00:07:58,639
energy to drive the ship 300,000 miles.

65
00:07:58,639 --> 00:08:02,300
Just how would this new source of power propel the Savannah?

66
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:06,579
Basically, this is how it works.

67
00:08:06,579 --> 00:08:11,699
Nuclear energy in the form of great heat is generated when control rods are withdrawn

68
00:08:11,699 --> 00:08:14,099
from between the fuel elements.

69
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:21,439
The higher the rods are raised, the greater the reactivity between the elements, and consequently,

70
00:08:21,439 --> 00:08:25,140
the greater the heat.

71
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:30,400
The intense heat of the core is carried off by water at a temperature of about 500 degrees

72
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:34,700
Fahrenheit in this pressurized system.

73
00:08:34,700 --> 00:08:39,580
This primary system water moves into a heat exchanger where it transfers its heat to water

74
00:08:39,580 --> 00:08:43,500
of a secondary system.

75
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:50,940
secondary water boils rapidly to create great amounts of steam. The steam drives

76
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:58,399
a turbine which turns the shaft of the propeller. Cold seawater condenses the

77
00:08:58,399 --> 00:09:07,179
steam back to water which is then pumped back to the heat exchanger. Around the

78
00:09:07,179 --> 00:09:13,059
reactor a complex system of shielding is constructed to assure utmost safety.

79
00:09:13,060 --> 00:09:17,860
The shielding consists of many types of material.

80
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:33,840
There are collision mats made up of alternate layers of thick steel and redwood planks.

81
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:38,780
Tons of concrete are poured to form a four foot barrier.

82
00:09:38,779 --> 00:09:44,419
From the top of this barrier, workmen apply polyethylene sheets to the heavy lead on the

83
00:09:44,419 --> 00:09:48,720
upper half of the containment vessel.

84
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:53,620
With this protection, the NS Savannah can withstand almost any conceivable collision

85
00:09:53,620 --> 00:09:58,819
with the world's largest merchant ships without any damage to her reactor.

86
00:09:58,819 --> 00:10:05,039
This makes her one of the safest ships afloat.

87
00:10:05,039 --> 00:10:08,719
The same care which goes into the reactor also goes into every one of the Savannah's

88
00:10:08,719 --> 00:10:12,659
other parts.

89
00:10:12,659 --> 00:10:18,439
Like this giant turbine manufactured by the DeLaval Steam Turbine Company.

90
00:10:18,439 --> 00:10:28,759
The precision of a jeweler is required in the making and testing of her massive years.

91
00:10:28,759 --> 00:10:34,399
The design and manufacture of every item is checked and tested.

92
00:10:34,399 --> 00:10:44,240
No flaw must escape detection, but for a great ship, machinery must be matched by men, men

93
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:51,639
the likes of whom have always made up America's great merchant crews.

94
00:10:51,639 --> 00:10:56,259
The Savannah's crew was drawn from among the finest, including graduates of the US Merchant

95
00:10:56,259 --> 00:11:01,559
Marine Academy at Kings Point, New York.

96
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:08,000
The ship's operator has assigned highly experienced officers and engineers to the NS Savannah.

97
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,340
All her engineers and senior deck officers have been required to take extensive training

98
00:11:12,340 --> 00:11:18,100
in new fields in order to operate a nuclear ship.

99
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:21,960
Included in the early classes were engineers of several foreign nations who were welcome

100
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:25,460
to share our knowledge of the peaceful atom.

101
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:28,100
Early training took place at Lynchburg, Virginia.

102
00:11:28,100 --> 00:11:34,540
Later, the academic work was given at the Merchant Marine Academy and at other reactor and industrial sites across the country.

103
00:11:35,580 --> 00:11:45,139
During a 15-month course, the men studied such subjects as nuclear physics, reactor theory and instrumentation, chemistry, thermodynamics, and health physics.

104
00:11:46,759 --> 00:11:52,100
To apply their theoretical training, students used an exact duplicate of the Savannah's control room.

105
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:58,700
realistic reactor operations were performed and evaluated with the aid of this reactor simulator

106
00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:13,620
at the shipyard a full-size mock-up of the entire reactor was constructed to facilitate

107
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:18,720
machinery installation on the ship and to help train the savannahs crew in maintenance procedures

108
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:28,440
There was no doubt that the crew of the NS Savannah would be among the most thoroughly trained ever assembled for a single ship.

109
00:12:36,460 --> 00:12:39,259
The construction of that ship was nearing completion.

110
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:51,000
Engineers and scientists of foreign countries were invited to see her construction and to study her many innovations.

111
00:12:52,100 --> 00:13:13,000
Among the ship's features were retractable stabilizers, which were designed to counter

112
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:18,019
any role in a heavy sea.

113
00:13:18,019 --> 00:13:47,620
One of the many great days for the Savannah had arrived, the day of her launching.

114
00:13:47,620 --> 00:13:51,019
name, she was now the nuclear ship Savannah.

115
00:13:57,120 --> 00:13:59,980
She would soon be that, in fact.

116
00:14:14,659 --> 00:14:19,340
With the containment vessel now ready to receive the reactor, a test assembly begins.

117
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:26,679
Various reactor components are carefully lowered, eased into place.

118
00:14:28,620 --> 00:14:32,320
This assembly is made under conditions known as be clean

119
00:14:32,320 --> 00:14:35,820
